description:
Every document program needs the same pieces around its menus: a table of commands with their shortcuts and state, turning key presses into commands (a run-time accelerator table), and a "recent files" list in the File menu. Write them without any windows, so the window procedure just asks them.

```cpp
struct Key { int vk; bool ctrl{ false }, shift{ false }, alt{ false }; };

class CommandTable
{
public:
    bool add(int id, const std::string& text, const std::string& shortcut);
    void setEnabled(int id, bool enabled);
    void setChecked(int id, bool checked);
    int translate(const Key& k) const;
    std::string menuLine(int id) const;
};

class RecentFiles
{
public:
    explicit RecentFiles(std::size_t max);                 // at most 9
    void add(const std::string& path);
    void remove(const std::string& path);
    const std::vector<std::string>& items() const;
    std::vector<std::string> menuItems(std::size_t maxChars) const;
    static std::string shorten(const std::string& path, std::size_t maxChars);
    std::string save() const;
    void load(const std::string& text);
};
```

**CommandTable**
- `add` fails (returns false) if the id is already used, the shortcut does not parse, or another command already has the same shortcut. An empty shortcut means none. Shortcuts use practice 2.04's rules (modifiers Ctrl/Control, Shift, Alt; letters, digits, F1-F12 and the named keys), and commands start enabled and unchecked.
- `translate` returns the id of the **enabled** command whose shortcut is exactly this key with exactly these modifiers, or 0.
- `menuLine` is the text, then a tab and the shortcut in practice 2.04's canonical form if there is one; with `"[x] "` in front when checked, and `" (disabled)"` at the end when disabled. Unknown ids give `""`.

**RecentFiles**
- `add` puts the path at the front; if it is already in the list (compared **case-insensitively**, as Windows paths are), the old entry is removed first. The list is cut to `max`.
- `remove` removes a path (case-insensitively), if present.
- `shorten` leaves paths of at most `maxChars` characters alone. Otherwise, with the path split on `\`, it keeps the first part (such as `C:`), then `\...\`, then as many of the **last** parts as fit, preferring to keep more; if not even the file name fits that way, it returns `...` followed by the last `maxChars - 3` characters.
- `menuItems` numbers the entries `&1 `, `&2 `, ... followed by the shortened path, with every `&` in the path doubled (`&&`), since a single one would become a mnemonic.
- `save` writes one path per line, each ending in `\n`; `load` replaces the list with the non-empty lines of a text (`\r\n` allowed), dropping later duplicates and anything beyond `max`.

